Pop the call-return frame an IRQ-continuation bridge returns through, and
drop frames left above an IRQ's entry depth when runtime_irq() returns.

A generated function inside an IRQ handler that calls (BL) a function with
no static entry leaks one generated call-return frame per call:

1. The BL pushes its return address (runtime_call_push_return), then the
   callee misses and runtime_bridge_interpret runs it in IRQ-continuation
   mode (g_irq_nest_depth != 0).
2. That mode ignores the stop PC and heals to static at the first PC with a
   static entry. When the callee returns, that PC is the BL's return
   address, which has an entry whenever static_resume_all is on or a branch
   targets it. The bridge breaks there without popping the frame (the
   no-pop rule for IRQ continuations after the loop).
3. Control returns to the BL site, which sees R15 == its return address and
   continues natively. Nothing pops the frame.
4. runtime_irq() raises and restores g_call_return_floor around the handler
   but never restores g_call_return_depth, so the frame survives the iret.
   The resumable path does restore it (runtime_exception_return,
   g_call_return_depth = frame.return_depth).

The stack then grows by one entry per such IRQ until "generated call-return
stack overflow" aborts the run (kCallReturnStackSize, 1024). Before that, a
leaked frame can become the stop PC of a later mainline bridge (the top of
the stack, when the bridge is entered at a nonzero depth). If that address
only ever runs in IRQ mode, the bridge interprets the main program until
kBridgeIterationCap (200M instructions) and aborts.

Fix, in two parts:

(a) When an IRQ-continuation bridge heals at a static entry, call
    runtime_call_should_return(pc), as a generated return would: it pops a
    matching frame above the IRQ floor, and does nothing when none matches.
(b) At the end of runtime_irq(), truncate g_call_return_depth to its value
    at handler entry, as the resumable path does. No host frame of the
    handler survives its iret, so no frame above that depth can still be
    returned to. A message is printed for the 1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th...
    truncation, as for call-return evictions.

With both parts, part (b) never fired in any run listed below: part (a)
leaves nothing for it to drop.

Known ambiguity, not new: part (a) matches return addresses by value, as
runtime_call_should_return already does for generated returns, so a heal at
a loop head equal to a pending return address pops that frame early.

Tested on macOS arm64 (Apple clang 21) at gbarecomp 5e89943 plus this
series:

- gbarecomp's ctest: 52 of 52 pass (with the gba-tests test ROMs).
- Two synthetic 4 KiB ROMs (no Nintendo data, header logo zero, run with
  --bios-skip-intro and GBARECOMP_SELFHEAL_RECOMPILE=0 for 2000 frames):
  a VBlank handler at 0x08000180 does `bl` to a routine that tail-calls,
  through a pointer in EWRAM, a function with no static entry.
  - With static_resume_all = true (the return address 0x08000188 has an
    entry): without this patch, "generated call-return stack overflow at
    return_pc=0x08000188", exit 134. With it, 2000 frames, exit 0, 2,000
    interpreted instructions (the callee, once per IRQ).
  - With static_resume_all = false, the BL returning to a loop label that
    a branch targets, and a main loop that calls VBlankIntrWait: without
    this patch, the bridge for the main loop's return address takes a
    leaked frame as its stop PC, and the run ends with the same overflow
    at return_pc=0x08000194, exit 134. With it, 2000 frames, exit 0,
    4,001 interpreted instructions.
  Both give the same results on the retail BIOS and on a minimal stand-in
  BIOS (vectors, IRQ dispatcher, VBlankIntrWait). The generators are
  tests/repro/make_rom.py and make_bios.py in the repository that carries
  this patch.
- A commercial game whose IRQ handler calls an IWRAM sound mixer through a
  ROM veneer, with the mixer not statically recompiled (the case that
  found the bug), headless, healing off: without this patch, a 3,600-frame
  run aborts with "SELF-HEAL bridge for 0x08003E70 exceeded 200000000
  instructions without returning to stop_pc=0x08004E10". With it, runs of
  2,700, 3,600, 5,400 and 12,000 frames exit 0, and the instructions
  interpreted in 2,700 frames fall from 228,577,755 to 119,865,640.

diff --git a/src/armv4t/runtime_arm.cpp b/src/armv4t/runtime_arm.cpp
index 3d07d55..ba03741 100644
--- a/src/armv4t/runtime_arm.cpp
+++ b/src/armv4t/runtime_arm.cpp
@@ -1523,6 +1523,7 @@ extern "C" void runtime_irq(uint32_t return_address) {
     // enclosing IRQ's) frames — see g_call_return_floor. Restored below.
     uint32_t saved_floor   = g_call_return_floor;
     g_call_return_floor    = g_call_return_depth;
+    const uint32_t entry_return_depth = g_call_return_depth;
     if (g_resumable_context) {
         gbarecomp::RuntimeIrqContinuation frame;
         frame.preserved = {saved_r0, saved_r1, saved_r2, saved_r3, saved_r12};
@@ -1572,6 +1573,26 @@ extern "C" void runtime_irq(uint32_t return_address) {
     g_cpu.R[12] = saved_r12;
     g_irq_iret_depth = saved_iret;  // restore an enclosing IRQ's expectation
     g_call_return_floor = saved_floor;
+    // No host frame of the handler survives its iret, so no call-return frame
+    // pushed above the entry depth can still be returned to. Drop any left
+    // over (e.g. by an interpreter bridge) so that none can become the stop PC
+    // of a later mainline bridge. The resumable path does the same in
+    // runtime_exception_return (g_call_return_depth = frame.return_depth).
+    if (g_call_return_depth > entry_return_depth) {
+        static unsigned long long dropped_events = 0;
+        ++dropped_events;
+        if ((dropped_events & (dropped_events - 1u)) == 0)
+            std::fprintf(stderr,
+                         "runtime_irq: dropped %u call-return frame(s) left "
+                         "above the IRQ entry depth %u (top return_pc=0x%08X, "
+                         "event #%llu)\n",
+                         g_call_return_depth - entry_return_depth,
+                         entry_return_depth,
+                         g_call_return_stack[g_call_return_depth - 1u],
+                         dropped_events);
+        g_call_return_depth = entry_return_depth;
+        clamp_call_return_hostless();
+    }
     --g_irq_nest_depth;
 }
 
diff --git a/src/runtime/runtime_arm_default_aborts.cpp b/src/runtime/runtime_arm_default_aborts.cpp
index 4b16bf4..e2b3adb 100644
--- a/src/runtime/runtime_arm_default_aborts.cpp
+++ b/src/runtime/runtime_arm_default_aborts.cpp
@@ -639,6 +639,13 @@ extern "C" int runtime_bridge_interpret(uint32_t entry_pc, bool entry_thumb,
         // runtime_irq()'s drive loop.
         if ((irq_cont || top_level) && (cpu.R[15] & ~1u) != entry_pc &&
             runtime_has_static_entry(cpu.R[15], cpu.thumb ? 1 : 0)) {
+            // An IRQ continuation that heals at a live call-return frame has
+            // just performed that frame's guest return in the interpreter.
+            // Consume the frame as a generated return would
+            // (runtime_call_should_return scans only down to the IRQ floor);
+            // otherwise the waiting caller resumes with its frame still
+            // pushed and every such call leaks one entry.
+            if (irq_cont) runtime_call_should_return(cpu.R[15]);
             break;
         }
 
@@ -672,7 +679,8 @@ extern "C" int runtime_bridge_interpret(uint32_t entry_pc, bool entry_thumb,
     bus->set_bios_access_enabled(g_cpu.R[15] < 0x00004000u);
     // An IRQ continuation that healed to static is not a subroutine return —
